# Build context exclusions for `app/` (the Dockerfile's context is ./app, per docker-compose.yml).
#
# The `builder` stage does `COPY . .`, so anything left here is baked into an image layer. Two of
# these matter for more than speed.

# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `app/.env` holds PAYLOAD_SECRET and the database password. Without this rule `COPY . .` bakes it
# into the builder layer of every image build — and layers outlive the file, survive `docker history`,
# and travel with a pushed or shared build cache. The Rock happens to have no `app/.env` today, so
# nothing has leaked; that is luck, not design, and it breaks the moment anyone creates one on a
# machine that also builds images.
#
# Nothing in the build needs it: the Rock builds with no `app/.env` at all (verified — its deploys
# succeed), and the runtime gets DATABASE_URI from compose's `env_file: .env` at the REPO ROOT, which
# is outside this context entirely. This also removes a local footgun: a developer whose `app/.env`
# points at `127.0.0.1` for host-run `next dev` (see AGENTS.md → Local stack) would otherwise have
# that unreachable-from-inside-a-container value copied into the builder.
.env
.env.*

# ── Build artefacts and installed trees ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `node_modules` is replaced wholesale by `COPY --from=deps`, and `.next` is regenerated by the build
# — copying either only slows the context upload (they are the two largest directories here).
node_modules
.next

# Test output, never an input.
playwright-report
test-results

# ── ⚑ The stray `app/app/` directory — this one silently destroys the build ──────────────────────
# This project's App Router lives at `src/app`. Next.js prefers a root-level `./app` over `./src/app`
# when BOTH exist, so an empty `Lesson3/app/app/` makes `next build` emit a build with ZERO
# application routes — no `/login`, no `/api/[...slug]`, nothing — and still exit 0. The served
# container then 500s every request with a ChunkLoadError from `_not-found`, which points at the
# bundler and not at the cause. That cost a session's worth of misdiagnosis, recorded as "the probe's
# app image builds a broken standalone bundle".
#
# It is created BY ACCIDENT and is invisible: the AGENTS.md probe recipe runs
# `docker run -v "$PWD/app:/app" -v /app/node_modules …` from the REPO ROOT, and running it from
# inside `app/` instead makes Docker CREATE `Lesson3/app/app/node_modules` on the host. Git cannot
# track an empty directory, so `git status` stays clean, the Rock and CI never see it, and only local
# image builds break — which is exactly the shape that reads as "works everywhere except here".
#
# Ignoring it here means an image build cannot be poisoned by it even when it exists. ⚑ If this
# project ever genuinely moves its App Router to a root-level `app/`, this rule must be DELETED, or
# the same zero-route build returns with the cause hidden one layer deeper.
/app

# ── Deliberately NOT excluded ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `src/` — the build needs it, and so does the `migrate` service, which runs from the `builder` stage
#   because the slim runtime image has no Payload CLI.
# `tests/` and `scripts/` — `next build` type-checks the project, and CI's e2e stage takes its source
#   from a BIND MOUNT rather than the image, so excluding them buys nothing and risks a build-time
#   type error for no benefit.
